Reviewer 2
Basic reporting
1. Literature gap: novelty framing needs repositioning against uncited recent work on safe/exposure-risk-based reranking, item-centric cold-start exploration, and cascading-bandit reward extensions — add explicit differentiation in Introduction/Related Work.

2. Equations (Sec. 3.1–3.4): mathematically correct but unnumbered and never cross-referenced — number them given eight distinct formulas. a, b (click model), τ, and n_rel are defined only symbolically — state the actual values used (e.g., τ=50, per your own repo defaults), matching the specificity already given for λ, η, m, top_L, α.

3. Table 2's repeated all-zero "Baseline" rows per penalty are redundant — state once, pivot to a wide format (penalty rows × strategy columns). Tables 3–6 share one η×m grid differing only by metric/penalty — merge into a single table with (a)–(d) sub-panels. Also include p=0.40 — the sharpest baseline-vs-EDE transition point, present in your artifacts but not the manuscript.

4. Figure presentation/uniformity: standardize DPI across all figure-generation scripts (currently 160/200/300), unify marker shapes and color-to-strategy mapping across every multi-series figure, add explicit (a)/(b) panel labels directly in-figure rather than relying on captions, unify axis-label/legend font sizes, and fix the overlapping λ=0.05/0.10 point labels in Fig. 2.

5.Data Availability, Conflict-of-Interest, and Funding statements are not visible in the manuscript body — confirm filed via portal or add to text. The Sec. 3.6 AI-use disclosure reads narrower in scope than the supplementary prompt log suggests (code-generation assistance spans most analysis modules) — recalibrate the wording.

6. Two reference entries (NeurIPS proceedings, textbook) carry a URL but no DOI — confirm acceptable under journal style.

Experimental design
Scope: squarely within PeerJ CS aims (AI Application/Research Article) — no concern.

1. Reproducibility link (Sec. 3.5): the repository DOI is an invisible hyperlink under "here" — state the DOI/URL as visible text.

2. Python/hardware are specified but package versions (NumPy/SciPy/etc.) are not pinned — add a dependency list.

3. "Grace period" and "hard-mode" (Sec. 3.3) are defined only qualitatively — give numeric definitions.

4. Sensitivity sweep (Tables 3–6) stops at η=0.65, which is also the tuned operating point's own value — extend the range to confirm gains aren't still rising.

5. Table 1's tuned point uses m=1 while the Fig. 1 difficulty sweep uses m=2 "for quality stability" — clarify why the two headline results use non-matching settings.

6.The real-log replay (Sec. 4.6) is a valuable safety check, but by the paper's own note that Yandex logs lack cold-start ground truth, it demonstrates warm-start minimal-disruption rather than cold-start discovery — reframe the section (and any title/intro claims) to state this scope explicitly.

Validity of the findings
1. Tables 1–2 report only point-estimate deltas though Sec. 3.4 promises mean±SD, and your own artifacts already contain per-config SD (roughly 4× the reported ΔNDCG) — add variance/CI and a paired significance test so readers can judge whether the NDCG differences are real.

2. Same gap applies to the real-log replay (Sec. 4.6/Fig. 4): bootstrap 95% CIs are stated as used (Sec. 3.5) but not shown for the reported position-change/engagement deltas — report them.

3. Sec. 4.6 states ≈97% click-preservation acceptance; Fig. 4's corresponding bar reads visually closer to ~99–100% — confirm the exact value matches the figure.

4. An EDE-Unsmooth (α=0) ablation is run across all conditions in your supplementary materials/results but never reported — include it or state why it's excluded; it is the most direct test of whether smoothing itself matters.

Additional comments
1. Simulation design (20 seeds, 3 click models, operating-point sweep) is methodologically solid — a genuine strength.

2. Minor language polish throughout; keep figure-caption punctuation consistent.

